name: Mastodon work session - audit the account-record contract before a rework
repo: mastodon
contract_symbol: Account
contract_file: app/models/account.rb
# TWO-STEP PROBE SCENARIO (plans/01-author.md). It exists so the next phase can run the
# mini-bench - both arms, once, at the real wall, unscored - against a real baseline.
# Nothing here is a verdict: no delta is claimed and none has been measured on this shape.
#
# Single-prompt session bench (bench/drivers/bench-sense-local.sh): every step is presented
# in ONE prompt and worked in order. Rendered to the agent: the description + each step's
# name and prompt ONLY (checks and gold are NEVER shown). Everything rendered is kept
# NEUTRAL - it states the task, never the answer's shape, file paths, class or method
# names, counts, or which tool to reach for.
#
# THE CONTRACT. `Account` (app/models/account.rb) is the record every other model,
# service, worker and controller in Mastodon hangs off: local users and remote actors
# are both rows in it, and it carries the lookup surface (named scopes, finders,
# aggregates and the mention pattern constant) that the rest of the codebase queries.
# It is the thing a maintainer reworking identity storage would actually open first.
#
# SEAM PROFILE (bench/lib/seam_hunt.py, `Account --file app/models/account.rb`):
#   app caller files : 107   (total incl test/non-app: 107)
#   SCATTER (dirs)   : 34
#   GREP-NOISE       : 'Account' in 257 app files   PRECISION=0.42
#   VERDICT          : SCATTERED + GREP-NOISY
# Ranked against the two other candidates profiled this session, lowest precision first:
#   Account                        0.42  (107 callers / 257 grep files, 34 dirs)  <- chosen
#   ActivityPub::TagManager        0.53  (75 callers / 141 grep files, 22 dirs)
#   ApplicationPolicy              0.53 REJECTED - the caller edges are false. Its
#     "callers" are controllers that define their own `current_user` / `user_signed_in?`,
#     the same method names ApplicationPolicy declares privately; checked by hand,
#     `grep -n 'Policy' app/controllers/about_controller.rb` and
#     `app/controllers/concerns/localized.rb` return NOTHING. A profile is not a caller
#     set until a sample of it is opened.
# Two more were profiled and are RESOLVER-GAP for this axis, recorded not hidden:
# `Redisable` and `Paginable` return 0 app caller files from seam_hunt; the MCP blast
# does show `Redisable` as 65 `includes Redisable` edges, but every one of those
# dependents writes the token on line 3, so its precision is ~1.0 - the near-transcription
# profile that has never separated arms.
# PRECISION RANKS, IT NEVER KILLS: the two-arm mini-bench next phase is what decides.
#
# THE AXIS (the whole thesis, and what the mini-bench measures). A dependent of this
# contract usually never holds an account object. It reaches the record class itself on
# ONE line - a named scope, a finder, an aggregate, or a shared pattern constant declared
# on the class - inside a file whose subject is something else entirely: a text-length
# check, a disk-usage figure, an index rebuild, a moderation sweep, a request-signature
# test, an admin log filter. The dependency is a single class-level call in a file about
# another job, so it is invisible in a reading of the model and unremarkable in a reading
# of the dependent.
#
# WHAT A GREP PRINTS, RECORDED AND NEVER A GATE (laws.md: NO GREP SCREEN IS A GATE, IN
# ANY FORM). The token is the repo's most common word: it is a column (`account_id`), a
# parameter, a local variable, an association (`belongs_to :account`) and a namespace
# (`AccountsController`, `AccountSuggestions`) in files with no dependency on the class
# at all. `Account` appears in 257 app files against 107 real caller files - precision
# 0.42, the lowest of the candidates profiled. That number ORDERS the candidates; it
# does not decide, and the run does.
#
# WHAT THE ARM IS SHOWN. `sense_blast Account` is AMBIGUOUS at MCP defaults (13 symbols
# named Account; the tool returns the hint and the top matches, so an arm pays one extra
# call to disambiguate). `sense_blast Account --file app/models/account.rb` returns 60
# direct callers over 30 areas, risk high, truncated. EVERY one of the 16 `dependents`
# rows below appears in that shown set; each row's ref was then opened by hand, because
# the blast payload gives the enclosing `def`, not the line that touches the contract.
#
# MEMORIZATION (memorization_probe.py, per row). CUT as recited: `InstancePresenter`
# (0.842, 16 members named) and `FeedManager` (0.818, 18 members named) - both are real,
# shown, in-pool dependents and both are dropped anyway. Every surviving row scored
# `not recited` or declared too few members to score. The anchor itself comes back
# recited (`Account` 0.312, 29 members) - that is the contract's own API surface, which
# is why the contract centre is a NON-SCORING anchor group here; no gold row's credit is
# a member list, each is a line-level fact at a path the probe never produces.
description: |
  You are a maintainer about to rework how a user account record is stored and looked
  up - the record that both local users and remote actors are rows in, and the lookup
  surface the rest of the codebase queries it through. Before you touch it, audit what
  depends on that contract, completely enough that the rework cannot silently break a
  dependent. Do not stop at the obvious half - the account model, the account
  controllers, the account services and the things whose own name is about accounts.
  A dependent counts wherever the record class itself is reached to make a decision, and
  the ones that matter live in files that are about something else. For each one give an
  exact file:line a teammate could jump straight to, plus one phrase on how it uses the
  contract. A filename with no line number does not count, and a missed dependent is a
  regression shipped.
steps:
- name: Map the account-record contract
  prompt: "Task: orient. In a few sentences plus exact file:line references, where does
    the account record live, what is the lookup surface it exposes to the rest of the
    codebase, and what would a rework of it have to preserve? Give file:line for
    everything you name.
    "
  checks:
  - type: response_richness
    value: '3'
    required: true
  - type: mcp_tool_used
    value: sense_graph
    required: false
    layer: adoption
- name: Audit every dependent of the account-record contract
  prompt: "Task: this is the core of the ticket. Find EVERY place in the codebase that
    depends on the account-record contract, so the rework cannot silently break one. Be
    exhaustive and group them by area. Most dependents never hold one of these records:
    they reach the record class itself on a SINGLE line - a named scope, a finder, an
    aggregate, or a shared pattern constant declared on the class - inside a file whose
    subject is an entirely different job, so the dependency is unremarkable in a reading
    of that file and invisible in a reading of the model. The record's name is also one
    of the most common words in this codebase: it is a column, a parameter, a local
    variable, an association and a namespace in files that do not depend on the contract
    at all, so a text search for it returns mostly non-dependents and gives no way to
    tell them apart. For each dependent give file:line and one phrase on how it uses the
    contract. A filename with no line number does not count, and a missed dependent is a
    regression shipped.
    "
  checks:
  - type: response_richness
    value: '6'
    required: true
    description: Enumerated the scattered class-level dependent set, not just the account-named files
  - type: no_grep
    value: no_grep
    required: false
    layer: adoption
  - type: mcp_tool_used
    value: sense_blast
    required: false
    layer: adoption
scoring:
  weights:
    correctness: 0.70
    efficiency: 0.30
# Gold is the must-find audit of what depends on the account record. TWO groups:
#   - `contract` (4, NON-SCORING anchor): the record itself and the obvious centre both
#     arms reach. It does not discriminate and it is not meant to.
#   - `dependents` (16, THE discriminator): the scattered periphery, ONE row per FILE,
#     across 12 directories. Every row is in the shown `sense_blast` output above, and
#     every credit below was read off its own line by hand (gold_audit stamp/verify),
#     never taken from a script tally.
gold:
  # --- NON-SCORING anchors: the contract centre ---
  - {id: contract-model, group: contract, match: [models/account.rb], relation: "app/models/account.rb:65 `class Account < ApplicationRecord` - the account record itself, the contract under change"}
  - {id: c:status, group: contract, match: [models/status.rb], relation: "app/models/status.rb:77 `belongs_to :account, inverse_of: :statuses` - the content model hangs off the account association"}
  - {id: c:user, group: contract, match: [models/user.rb], relation: "app/models/user.rb:72 `belongs_to :account, inverse_of: :user` - a local login is a User row pointing at an Account row"}
  - {id: c:resolve-service, group: contract, match: [services/resolve_account_service.rb], relation: "app/services/resolve_account_service.rb:27 `@account ||= Account.find_remote(@username, @domain)` - the webfinger resolve path, the obvious lookup entry point for accounts"}
  # --- THE DISCRIMINATOR: one class-level reach per file, in a file about another job ---
  - {id: d:note-length-validator, group: dependents, match: [validators/note_length_validator.rb], relation: "app/validators/note_length_validator.rb:19 `new_text.gsub!(Account::MENTION_RE, '@\\2')` - a bio-length validator borrows the account class's mention pattern so a mention counts as its short form"}
  - {id: d:extractor, group: dependents, match: [lib/extractor.rb], relation: "app/lib/extractor.rb:28 `text.scan(Account::MENTION_RE) do |screen_name, _|` - the text entity extractor scans for mentions with the account class's pattern constant"}
  - {id: d:self-destruct-scheduler, group: dependents, match: [scheduler/self_destruct_scheduler.rb], relation: "app/workers/scheduler/self_destruct_scheduler.rb:54 `@inboxes ||= Account.inboxes` - the instance self-destruct sweep asks the account class for every delivery inbox to notify"}
  - {id: d:media-privacy-check, group: dependents, match: [system_check/media_privacy_check.rb], relation: "app/lib/admin/system_check/media_privacy_check.rb:79 `attachment = Account.representative.media_attachments.take` - the admin storage-privacy check reaches the instance's representative account to get a real attachment URL to probe"}
  - {id: d:space-usage-dimension, group: dependents, match: [dimension/space_usage_dimension.rb], relation: "app/lib/admin/metrics/dimension/space_usage_dimension.rb:44 `Account.sum(Arel.sql('COALESCE(avatar_file_size, 0) + COALESCE(header_file_size, 0)'))` - the admin disk-usage figure aggregates avatar and header sizes straight off the account table"}
  - {id: d:json-ld-helper, group: dependents, match: [helpers/json_ld_helper.rb], relation: "app/helpers/json_ld_helper.rb:233 `on_behalf_of ||= Account.representative` - unsigned fetches fall back to the instance representative account to sign the request with"}
  - {id: d:moderation-action, group: dependents, match: [admin/moderation_action.rb], relation: "app/models/admin/moderation_action.rb:85 `representative_account = Account.representative` - marking statuses sensitive attributes the moderation edit to the representative account"}
  - {id: d:action-log-filter, group: dependents, match: [admin/action_log_filter.rb], relation: "app/models/admin/action_log_filter.rb:114 `account = Account.find_or_initialize_by(id: value)` - filtering the audit log by target builds an unsaved account to match the polymorphic target on"}
  - {id: d:entity-cache, group: dependents, match: [lib/entity_cache.rb], relation: "app/lib/entity_cache.rb:15 `Account.select(:id, :username, :domain, :url).find_remote(username, domain)` - the mention entity cache uses the class finder with a narrowed column set"}
  - {id: d:accounts-index-importer, group: dependents, match: [importer/accounts_index_importer.rb], relation: "app/lib/importer/accounts_index_importer.rb:28 `Account.searchable` - the search index rebuild defines its import scope as the searchable account scope"}
  - {id: d:statuses-index-importer, group: dependents, match: [importer/statuses_index_importer.rb], relation: "app/lib/importer/statuses_index_importer.rb:62 `Mention.where(account: Account.local, silent: false).select(:id, :status_id)` - the status index rebuild narrows mentions to local accounts via the class scope"}
  - {id: d:suggestions-source, group: dependents, match: [account_suggestions/source.rb], relation: "app/models/account_suggestions/source.rb:13 `Account` then `.searchable.where(discoverable: true).without_silenced.without_memorial` - the follow-suggestion base scope is chained off the class"}
  - {id: d:friends-of-friends, group: dependents, match: [friends_of_friends_source.rb], relation: "app/models/account_suggestions/friends_of_friends_source.rb:10 `Account.find_by_sql([<<~SQL.squish, { id: account.id, limit: limit }])` - the friends-of-friends suggestion runs raw SQL through the account class"}
  - {id: d:account-batch-form, group: dependents, match: [form/account_batch.rb], relation: "app/models/form/account_batch.rb:69 `Account.where(id: account_ids)` - the admin bulk-action form resolves its checked ids back to records through the class"}
  - {id: d:signature-verification, group: dependents, match: [concerns/signature_verification.rb], relation: "app/controllers/concerns/signature_verification.rb:48 `signed_request_actor.is_a?(Account) ? signed_request_actor : nil` - HTTP signature verification type-tests the signing actor against the class before treating it as an account"}
  - {id: d:application-controller, group: dependents, match: [controllers/application_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/application_controller.rb:108 `@single_user_mode ||= Rails.configuration.x.single_user_mode && Account.without_internal.exists?` - the single-user-mode gate is an existence check over the non-internal account scope"}
